# Orchestration Spoonfeeder — <PROJECT_NAME>

> **Audience:** humans (engineers on the team). Copy-paste-friendly examples for actually using the framework day-to-day.

---

## Quick start: most common day-to-day tasks

### "I need to fix one thing in one view"

Just open the view file. Copilot Chat (with the file as active) auto-loads `.github/instructions/swiftui.instructions.md`. Ask in Chat: *"Update this view to use `@StateObject` instead of `@ObservedObject` for the model."*

No agent invocation needed. Inline Chat or full Chat panel both work.

### "I need to add a feature that touches data + UI + maybe network"

This is orchestrator territory. In Chat:

```
@<PROJECT_SLUG>-orchestrator add a streak counter to the profile screen.
The streak should increment on each app launch and persist across launches.
```

The orchestrator will:
1. Restate what you asked
2. Decompose into ordered handoffs (likely `ios-data` first, then `ios-ui`)
3. Issue handoffs one at a time, validate each return
4. Aggregate and report back

You stay in the Chat thread; the orchestrator drives.

### "I need to commit and push"

```
/commit-push-pr
```

The skill (works in the IDE, on the cloud agent and from `copilot -p`) will:
- Verify branch is not `main` / `develop`
- Run the build (if any iOS-build-relevant files are dirty)
- Refuse to stage `.cer` / `.p12` / `.mobileprovision` / `.env*`
- Generate a Conventional Commit message
- Push and open a PR against `<DEFAULT_BASE_BRANCH>`

If anything fails (build broken, signing material in working tree, etc.), it stops and tells you what to fix.

### "I just got a Crashlytics top-issue alert"

```
@ios-perf triage the top Crashlytics issue from the last 7 days
```

If you have the Sentry / Crashlytics MCP installed, the perf specialist will fetch real crash data, find the hotspot, and propose a fix. If you don't have the MCP, give it the crash ID + stack trace in the message.

### "I corrected Copilot on something"

After the correction, run:

```
/correction-capture
```

The skill walks Copilot through deciding whether the lesson is recurring enough to harden into an instruction file, drafts the patch, and asks for your approval before applying.

